  "summary": "Recent preprints apply selection and dynamical models to pneumococcal vaccine replacement, soil phage microdiversity, and in vitro phage-bacteria coexistence.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is how selection and phage-bacteria interactions structure genetic variation and population dynamics. A reusable Wright-Fisher-based model with negative frequency-dependent selection on the pneumococcal accessory genome explains vaccine replacement and guides surveillance timing across vaccine introductions. A dynamical-systems analysis of a K. pneumoniae-phage model calibrated in vitro finds that quasineutral equilibria govern clearance of resistant strains and shape coexistence. A soil study reports higher microdiversity in temperate than in virulent bacteriophages, distinguishing these lifestyles by within-population diversity. What is new is the explicit transfer of a selection framework to surveillance design for a bacterial population, while the phage studies address coexistence and clearance dynamics rather than surveillance.",
